<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<databaseChangeLog xmlns="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog"
                   xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance"
                   xsi:schemaLocation="http://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog https://www.liquibase.org/xml/ns/dbchangelog/dbchangelog-latest.xsd">

    <!--
    Size the two remaining URL-width file reference columns after a filename.

    These columns used to hold the URL a file is served under, so their width was a URL length: course.course_icon was
    255 and jhi_user.image_url was 256, and a REST rename that lengthened a path by one character had to widen the
    column. The preceding changeset reduced both to the filename they always ended with, so the width now follows from
    what FileUtil#generateFilename produces: at most 22 characters of type prefix, a 23 character timestamp, an
    underscore, 8 characters of UUID, a dot and at most 9 characters of extension, which is 64. Neither column can
    receive a name the user gave a file: a course icon and a profile picture are both written with keepFilename unset.
    100 leaves 36 characters of headroom and is the width exam_user.signing_image_path and exam_user.student_image_path
    already have, so all four columns whose value the server generates in full now share one number instead of four.
    FileUtil#GENERATED_FILENAME_MAX_LENGTH states the same number on the entity side and carries the arithmetic;
    StoredFileReferenceTest fails if a longer prefix or a newly allowed extension ever breaks the sum.

    The other four file reference columns keep their width. attachment.jhi_link, attachment.student_version,
    drag_and_drop_question.background_file_path and slide.slide_image_path are written with keepFilename set, so their
    value embeds the name the user gave the uploaded file, which nothing in this application bounds. Their width is a
    cap on how long a file may be named rather than a number derived from the code, and narrowing it would start
    rejecting uploads that succeed today.

    Narrowing a column fails on a row that no longer fits, and on MySQL outside strict mode it would silently truncate
    one instead, which for a file reference means losing the file. Both changesets are therefore guarded by a check
    that no such row exists. onFail="CONTINUE" rather than the default: a row that is too long must not stop the
    application from starting, and it must not mark the changeset as applied either, so the narrowing is retried on the
    next start once the row has been dealt with. In that case the column keeps its current width, which is wider than
    the entity declares; with ddl-auto set to none nothing validates the two against each other, so the application
    runs unchanged. char_length is used rather than length because MySQL's length counts bytes while the column counts
    characters; both databases spell char_length the same way.

    Rolling deployment: a node on the previous release writes the URL-shaped value back, and the longest of those is a
    course icon URL at about 65 characters and a profile picture URL at about 80, so such a write still fits. A node on
    this release reduces it to the filename the next time it reads it.

    The rollback widens each column back to the width it had.
    -->

    <changeSet id="20260907001339-01-course-icon-filename-width" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="CONTINUE" onFailMessage="course.course_icon still holds a value longer than 100 characters, leaving the column at its current width">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM course WHERE char_length(course_icon) > 100</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <modifyDataType tableName="course" columnName="course_icon" newDataType="varchar(100)"/>
        <rollback>
            <modifyDataType tableName="course" columnName="course_icon" newDataType="varchar(255)"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

    <changeSet id="20260907001339-02-user-image-url-filename-width" author="krusche">
        <preConditions onFail="CONTINUE" onFailMessage="jhi_user.image_url still holds a value longer than 100 characters, leaving the column at its current width">
            <sqlCheck expectedResult="0">SELECT COUNT(*) FROM jhi_user WHERE char_length(image_url) > 100</sqlCheck>
        </preConditions>
        <modifyDataType tableName="jhi_user" columnName="image_url" newDataType="varchar(100)"/>
        <rollback>
            <modifyDataType tableName="jhi_user" columnName="image_url" newDataType="varchar(256)"/>
        </rollback>
    </changeSet>

</databaseChangeLog>
